The situation you're postulating here (staying alive because you
rustproof base ac:1 items intead of enchanting things) doesn't exist
except for my previously mentioned boots of water walking (for
lava-walking) scenario or if your armor's already fully enchanted.

It's not a case of one being a good long-term strategy and the other
being for short-term survival strategy.  Assuming the enchantment on
your armor isn't already maxed out:

Enchanting your base ac:1 armor always gives you better AC in the
short-term than rustproofing it.
Enchanting your base ac:1 armor never gives you worse AC in the
long-term (after it's fully damaged) than fooproofing it, and on
average gives you better AC.

I'm not sure exactly what you're missing, so to clarify:
Rust (or corrosion/burning) never destroys weapons/armor (although lava
can).  It also never decreases their protection more than the base
value of the item itself.  So if you have, say, a thoroughly rusty
thoroughly corroded +2 gauntlets of power, they still give you 2 points
of AC.  The magical bonus is unaffected by damage.

Now with base ac2 items, the situation is slightly different.  But
there's only 1 of those (a robe) that isn't very easy to replace.  The
robe is sometimes worth fooproofing (esp. if all your stuff is better
than +1 already)  Dwarvish helms, iron shoes, and large shields are
abundant.  And, a blessed ?ea often gives 2-3 points of AC in which
case the same logic applies as with base AC1 items.

So with base AC2 items, if:
1. You only get 1 AC from the ?ea; and
2. The particular item you happen to have fooproofed is the one
targetted by the damage; and
3. It is damaged twice

Then you will have 1 more point of AC for the brief window of time
until you grab a replacement.

Except for robes (where grabbing a replacement isn't trivially easy), I
can't imagine that giving up at least one point of AC throughout the
game is worth that minimal tradeoff, especially since if you get 2-3
points of AC then you're still at least as well off even in that window
of time.

Now using c?destroy armor to fooproof is worthwile.



